ref('davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville',r'David, Fran{\c c}ois and Kupiainen, Antti and Rhodes, R{\'e}mi and Vargas, Vincent','Liouville Quantum Gravity on the Riemann Sphere',2016,'https://arxiv.org/pdf/1410.7318',journal='Communications in Mathematical Physics',volume=342,pages='869--907',doi='10.1007/s00220-016-2572-4',annote='Full primary arXiv PDF opened, especially Theorem 3.2 (Seiberg bounds), Definition 3.6 and Theorem 3.11 (Weyl anomaly). First arXiv version 27 October 2014; publisher metadata opened and confirms 3 February 2016 publication. This is the subcritical spacelike sphere theory, not every target dimension or genus sum.')
ref('zamolodchikov1982entropy',r'Zamolodchikov, A. B.','On the entropy of random surfaces',1982,'https://www.itp.ac.ru/en/persons/zamolodchikov-aleksandr-borisovich/',journal='Physics Letters B',volume=117,number='1--2',pages='87--90',doi='10.1016/0370-2693(82)90879-6',annote='Author institutional bibliography entry 109 and 1982 publication list opened. Publisher original full text was inaccessible. Metadata only; no exact theorem about the all-genus sum is inferred.')
talk(569,'1982-10-04','Al. B. Zamolodchikov','P001045-P001051','From path entropy to the Polyakov metric integral and Liouville theory',r'The actual entry starts halfway through P001047 and ends on P001049. Zamolodchikov reviews the proper-time representation of a particle path sum, then the fixed-topology Polyakov metric integral, quotienting by reparametrizations, functional metrics, conformal gauge and the Liouville action. The uncertainty about summing over handles is separate from the fixed-topology calculation. Several expressions are formal prescriptions: neither an infinite-dimensional Riemannian metric nor division by a gauge-group volume by itself constructs a measure.')
add(569,'Proper time and the particle Green function','P001047','method',r'Equations (2)--(2\textquotesingle) replace the length-weighted sum by an integral over \(\tau>0\) and Brownian paths with kinetic term \(\frac12\int\dot x^2dt\), giving \(\tau^{-d/2}\exp[-m^2\tau-R^2/(2\tau)]\).',r'For Brownian motion with generator \(\frac12\Delta\) on \(\mathbb R^d\), the heat kernel is \(p_\tau(R)=(2\pi\tau)^{-d/2}e^{-|R|^2/(2\tau)}\). For \(m^2>0\), its Laplace transform is the resolvent kernel of \(m^2-\frac12\Delta\), with Fourier multiplier \((m^2+|p|^2/2)^{-1}\). The length-weighted lattice sum reaches this expression only after a specified diffusive limit and mass renormalization.',r'This is the particle comparison underlying \citet[Eqs.~(1)--(5)]{polyakov1981quantum}; it repeats the preceding lecture in a consistent proper-time normalization.',('polyakov1981quantum',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='The normalized heat kernel, generator and positive-mass hypothesis are supplied. The formula does not assign ordinary arc length to Brownian paths.')
add(569,'Entropy subtraction and the renormalized mass','P001047','method',r'The number of length-\(L\) paths is written \(f(L)\sim L^\beta e^{\varkappa L}\), with \(\varkappa\) cutoff-dependent and \(\beta\) cutoff-independent; the notes conclude \(m^2=m_0-\varkappa\).',r'For a specified lattice-walk ensemble the exponential growth constant shifts the bare length penalty to \(m_0-\varkappa\). A Brownian continuum limit additionally rescales the number of steps as inverse step length squared and fixes the diffusion coefficient. Consequently the numerical identity \(m^2=m_0-\varkappa\) is schematic until the units of \(L\) and \(\tau\) and the cutoff scaling are specified. Polynomial exponents depend on dimension, endpoints and the walk ensemble.',r'\citet[Eqs.~(1)--(5)]{polyakov1981quantum} uses the same random-walk analogy. The entropy subtraction is standard; the notes do not state a universality theorem encompassing different path ensembles.',('polyakov1981quantum',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='The identification is kept as a scaling prescription. No self-avoiding-walk or arbitrary-regulator universality claim is supplied.')
add(569,'The remark about singular infinite-dimensional measures','P001047','remark',r'A margin says \ru{В \(\infty\)-мерном пр-ве почти \(\forall\) две меры взаимно сингулярны}.',r'There is no specified class of measures or measure on that class, so the phrase almost every pair has no definite quantifier. Infinite-dimensional Gaussian measures can be equivalent or mutually singular depending on their means and covariance operators; dimension alone does not decide the comparison.',r'The statement cannot be assigned a precise probability theorem from the notebook. It serves as a warning that changing a continuum regularization can change the measure class.',cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',reconstruction='No particular Gaussian-equivalence criterion is attributed to the speaker.',status='an underdetermined heuristic, not an identified open problem.')
add(569,'The fixed-topology Polyakov metric integral','P001048','construction',r'Equation (5) uses maps \(x(\xi)\), an independent positive metric \(g\), a Gaussian kinetic action and the area term \(\mu_0^2\int\sqrt g\); the topology is explicitly fixed.',r'For a fixed oriented surface \(\Sigma\), the formal bosonic-string action is \(S[x,g]=\frac12\int_\Sigma |dx|_g^2\,dA_g+\mu_0^2\int_\Sigma dA_g\). Maps and metrics are integrated modulo the chosen diffeomorphism gauge; conformal anomaly and metric moduli remain part of the definition. Equality with a combinatorial sum over embedded surfaces requires a regulator and a scaling-limit theorem, neither specified here.',r'\citet[Eqs.~(3)--(10)]{polyakov1981quantum}, published 23 July 1981, gives this metric-integral formulation. The notebook reports an existing formal construction, not an established equality of all possible random-surface measures.',('polyakov1981quantum',),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Fixed topology, positivity and formal status are made explicit.')
add(569,'Could surfaces with many handles dominate?','P001048','question',r'The margin asks about the relative contributions of handles and holes, suggesting that \ru{Пенистые пов.} with many handles may dominate.',r'An all-topology sum requires a class of surfaces, boundary data, a regulator, a weight for each genus, and a summation or convergence criterion. These are not fixed, so the statement does not select a definite theorem about large-genus asymptotics or a nonperturbative string partition function.',r'Zamolodchikov\textquotesingle s \citet{zamolodchikov1982entropy} is a genuine 1982 publication on random-surface entropy; its existence does not identify a solution of this unspecified all-genus question. The modern fixed-sphere construction \citet{davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville} likewise has a different scope.',('zamolodchikov1982entropy','davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville'),cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction='No genus fugacity or limiting ensemble is invented.',confidence='medium',status='the historical question remains underdetermined; no open-problem identifier is assigned.')
add(569,'Functional metrics and the trace direction','P001048','definition',r'Equation (6) gives \(\|\delta x\|^2=\int\sqrt g\,|\delta x|^2\) and \(\|\delta g\|^2=\int\sqrt g\,(g^{aa\prime}g^{bb\prime}+c\,g^{ab}g^{a\prime b\prime})\delta g_{ab}\delta g_{a\prime b\prime}\).',r'For a symmetric variation \(h\) of a two-dimensional positive metric, write \(h=h_0+\frac12(\operatorname{tr}_g h)g\). The second integrand equals \(|h_0|_g^2+(c+\frac12)(\operatorname{tr}_g h)^2\). It defines a positive inner product on all metric variations exactly when \(c>-\frac12\); equality is degenerate and smaller values give an indefinite trace direction.',r'The invariant functional-metric prescription is in \citet[Eqs.~(6)--(10)]{polyakov1981quantum}. The positivity condition follows by the displayed trace decomposition, an elementary check \ours.',('polyakov1981quantum',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='The omitted condition on c is supplied. A positive field-space norm still does not create a countably additive flat volume measure.')
add(569,'Diffeomorphisms and the covector connection sign','P001048','construction',r'Equation (7) gives \(\delta g_{ab}=\nabla_a\varepsilon_b+\nabla_b\varepsilon_a\), but defines \(\nabla_a\varepsilon_b=\partial_a\varepsilon_b+\Gamma^c_{ab}\varepsilon_c\).',r'For the Levi--Civita Christoffel symbols, a covector satisfies \(\nabla_a\varepsilon_b=\partial_a\varepsilon_b-\Gamma^c_{ab}\varepsilon_c\). With \(\varepsilon_b=g_{bc}\varepsilon^c\), the metric Lie derivative is \((\mathcal L_\varepsilon g)_{ab}=\nabla_a\varepsilon_b+\nabla_b\varepsilon_a\). The plus sign belongs to the connection on contravariant components.',r'This is the gauge variation used in \citet[Eqs.~(7)--(10)]{polyakov1981quantum}. The discrepancy is a faithful notebook sign error, confirmed on the scan.',('polyakov1981quantum',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The usual Levi--Civita convention is fixed; the inverse coordinate-change convention could reverse the whole Lie derivative, but not only its Christoffel term.')
add(569,'Does dividing by gauge volume define the measure?','P001048','claim',r'After \(d\mu(x,g)=\mathcal Dx\,\mathcal Dg/\mathcal D\omega\), the notes say \ru{Теперь интеграл (5) определён хорошо}.',r'Functional metrics and a formal quotient are not by themselves a measure construction. One must control a regulator, zero modes, gauge stabilizers, renormalization and metric moduli. A precise later result is the construction of spacelike Liouville correlation functions on the sphere for \(0<\gamma<2\), \(\mu>0\), \(Q=2/\gamma+\gamma/2\), and insertions satisfying \(\alpha_i<Q\), \(\sum_i\alpha_i>2Q\). This is a specified model, not the unrestricted quotient integral.',r'\citet[Theorem~3.2 and Definition~3.6]{davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville}, first circulated 27 October 2014 and published in 2016, gives the indicated rigorous construction. The bare uninserted sphere integral has a divergent zero-mode direction; the notebook\textquotesingle s assertion is not justified by (6)--(9).',('davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville',),cl='B',literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='The later theorem is stated separately from the 1982 formal claim.',status='the stated subcritical sphere theory is constructed. The notes do not define a universal measure problem beyond that scope.')
add(569,'Conformal gauge, residual automorphisms and moduli','P001048','method',r'The representative is \(g_{ab}=\rho\,\delta_{ab}\), followed by \ru{Остаётся конформная группа (конечномерная)}.',r'Every smooth two-dimensional metric is locally conformally flat. Globally write \(g=e^\varphi\widehat g(m)\) using a reference metric in its conformal class and its moduli \(m\). For a fixed compact Riemann surface the global conformal automorphism group is finite-dimensional; germs of local conformal transformations are not. A single globally Euclidean chart does not cover an arbitrary closed surface.',r'\citet[Eqs.~(11)--(12) and discussion of moduli]{polyakov1981quantum} uses conformal gauge with global qualifications. The later sphere construction \citet[Section~3.2]{davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville} treats the residual Möbius action explicitly.',('polyakov1981quantum','davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville'),verification='read the result',reconstruction='The distinction between local coordinates, global automorphisms and moduli is added.')
add(569,'Matter and ghost determinants are different operators','P001048','claim',r'After Gaussian integration the notes say the determinants are identical and write \([\det\Delta_g]^{(26-d)/2}\) in equation (11).',r'Integrating \(d\) scalar matter fields gives a factor \((\det{}^{\prime}\Delta_g)^{-d/2}\), with the translation zero modes treated separately. Gauge fixing contributes the determinant of the vector-to-traceless-tensor operator \(P\varepsilon=\nabla\varepsilon+(\nabla\varepsilon)^t-g\,\operatorname{div}\varepsilon\), together with zero-mode and moduli factors. The ghost conformal anomaly has coefficient \(-26\); it is not an exact identity of its determinant with the thirteenth power of the scalar Laplacian determinant.',r'\citet[Eqs.~(13)--(24)]{polyakov1981quantum} distinguishes the operators before calculating their conformal variations. Equation (11) captures an anomaly coefficient while suppressing determinant-line, zero-mode and global factors.',('polyakov1981quantum',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='Primed determinants and the actual ghost operator replace the literal scalar-determinant identification.')
add(569,'The induced Liouville action and its scope','P001049','construction',r'Equation (12) sets \(\rho=e^\varphi\) and display the exponent \((d-26)S_L/(48\pi)\), with \(S_L=\int[\frac12(\partial\varphi)^2+m^2e^\varphi]\,d^2\xi\).',r'The Weyl-anomaly calculation induces a local kinetic term for the conformal factor and a renormalized area term. On a curved reference surface it also includes a background-curvature coupling. The rigorous subcritical spacelike normalization uses a renormalized exponential, \(Q=2/\gamma+\gamma/2\), and central charge \(c_L=1+6Q^2>25\). Cancellation with matter requires \(c_{\mathrm m}+c_L-26=0\); this regime corresponds to \(c_{\mathrm m}<1\), not every integer target dimension \(d<26\).',r'The formal induced action was already in \citet[Eqs.~(25)--(31)]{polyakov1981quantum}. The quantum parameter shift and rigorous Weyl anomaly are verified in \citet[Introduction and Theorem~3.11]{davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville}. These later results clarify a specified branch without turning the notebook\textquotesingle s formal expression into a theorem for all dimensions.',('polyakov1981quantum','davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville'),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='The curved-background term, quantum renormalization and restricted matter-charge range are modern qualifications.',status='the formal anomaly mechanism is established, and the stated spacelike sphere realization is rigorous; no blanket all-dimension claim is made.')
add(569,'The field-dependent norm of the conformal factor','P001049','definition',r'Equation (13) gives \(\|\delta\varphi\|^2=\int e^\varphi(\delta\varphi)^2\,d^2\xi\) as the metric behind \(d\mu(\varphi)\).',r'For \(g=e^\varphi\widehat g\), the induced trace-variation norm is proportional to \(\int_\Sigma e^\varphi(\delta\varphi)^2dA_{\widehat g}\), with a positive proportionality factor depending on the trace coefficient in (6). It is a field-dependent formal \(L^2\) metric. Its associated symbolic volume must not be replaced by flat \(\mathcal D\varphi\) without calculating a regulator-dependent Jacobian.',r'\citet[Eqs.~(29)--(31)]{polyakov1981quantum} explicitly retains this nontrivial metric in the Liouville integral. Modern probabilistic constructions begin from a Gaussian free field and renormalized multiplicative chaos instead of a literal Riemannian volume in infinitely many dimensions.',('polyakov1981quantum',),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='The proportionality constant and background volume form are restored.')
corr('P001047','Equation (4), relation between masses',r'\(m^2=m_0-\varkappa\).',r'Treat as a schematic entropy subtraction until the lattice spacing, proper-time rescaling and diffusion coefficient have been fixed.','Scan1047 inspected. Length and Brownian time scale differently; the text itself states regularization dependence.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001048','Equation (6), metric on metric variations',r'Unrestricted coefficient c in a squared norm.',r'Require \(c>-1/2\) in two dimensions for positivity on all symmetric variations.','Scan1048 inspected; trace/traceless decomposition gives |h0|²+(c+1/2)(tr h)².',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001048','Equation (7), covariant derivative',r'\(\nabla_a\varepsilon_b=\partial_a\varepsilon_b+\Gamma^c_{ab}\varepsilon_c\).',r'\(\nabla_a\varepsilon_b=\partial_a\varepsilon_b-\Gamma^c_{ab}\varepsilon_c\).','The scan and diplomatic Russian explicitly have plus. A covector carries the dual connection; direct metric-compatibility check requires minus.')
corr('P001048','Sentence after equations (8)--(9)',r'The integral is now well defined.',r'The quotient is a formal gauge-invariant prescription, pending regularization, zero-mode treatment and measure construction.','The scan agrees with the transcription; an infinite-dimensional field-space metric does not supply a locally finite flat volume. Later rigorous sphere construction requires renormalization and Seiberg bounds.')
corr('P001048','Equation (10), residual conformal group',r'Conformally flat representative on a plane; remaining group finite-dimensional.',r'Conformal flatness is local; retain conformal moduli and distinguish compact-surface global automorphisms from local conformal transformations.','Scan1048 inspected; the global and local statements have different hypotheses.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001048','Sentence and equation (11), equal determinants',r'Ghost and scalar determinants are identical; determinant exponent (26-d)/2.',r'The scalar and ghost operators differ. Their conformal anomalies combine with coefficient 26-d, while zero modes, determinant normalizations and moduli factors remain.','Scan1048 inspected. Polyakov1981 equations13--24 distinguish matter and ghost operators before anomaly calculation.')
audit.append('## Record 569\nRussian1047--1049 read and all three scans inspected. Exact boundaries: Zamolodchikov heading midway1047, sparse terminal1049, before Ginzburg1050. Polyakov1981 full original https://qft.itp.ac.ru/polyakov-2.pdf read, equations3--31 checked, including field-dependent conformal-factor norm. DKRV full https://arxiv.org/pdf/1410.7318 opened and Theorems3.2,3.11 plus Definition3.6 checked; publisher https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00220-016-2572-4 confirms CMP342869--907,3February2016. First circulation27October2014. Spacelike0<gamma<2, sphere, Seiberg bounds retained; no all-dimension or all-genus resolution claimed. Zamolodchikov entropy paper verified only through author institutional bibliography https://www.itp.ac.ru/en/persons/zamolodchikov-aleksandr-borisovich/ entry109 and1982institute bibliography; original ScienceDirect inaccessible. No precise all-handles problem reconstructed from unspecified weights. Direct trace decomposition checks positivity c>-1/2 and covector sign checked against scan. Previous index has broad path-integral and Liouville themes, no exact identified earlier problem ID for these unspecified measure questions.')
